<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Saint Barthélemy (Swedish)</h1><p class="st-meta">1784 CE – 1878 CE · capital Gustavia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Sweden's only long-lasting colony, a small Caribbean island whose free port of Gustavia boomed in the age of revolutionary wars.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1784 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iii">Gustav III</a> obtained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-barthelemy">Saint Barthélemy</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in exchange for French trading rights at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a>. The Swedes renamed the main harbour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavia">Le Carénage</a>, as Gustavia, after the king, and in 1785 declared it a free port.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A Swedish governor ran the colony, at first with the Swedish West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Company. During the wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavia">Gustavia</a> thrived as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> for goods, including enslaved people, and its population rose to several thousand. The island had little farmland and depended on trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> was abolished in 1847.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After peace returned, trade declined, and hurricanes and fires damaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavia">Gustavia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> sold the island back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by a treaty of 1877, after the inhabitants voted almost unanimously for the transfer, and it passed to France in 1878. Gustavia keeps its Swedish name and some Swedish buildings.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/saint-barthelemy-swedish.jpg" alt="Saint Barthélemy (Swedish)" width="320" height="229"><figcaption>Image: Lokal_Profil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svensk_flagg_1815.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1784 CE – 1878 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gustavia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gustav III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic and Protestant Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, English, Swedish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Swedish Saint Barthélemy, Saint-Barthélemy</dd></dl></aside></div>
